<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp2sec7" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Basis as Coordinate System</title>

<p>
  A <term>coordinate system</term> is an agreed upon rule to describe where points are relative to us.
  We care about ordered bases because they give us a new coordinate system to describe things.
  As we will see later, problems that seem difficult when looking at them relative to the standard coordinates become simple when viewed relative to a different, well-chosen coordinate system.
</p>

<insight xml:id="mental_act-coordinatization">
  <title>Coordinatization</title>
  <p>
    This book takes the perspective that objects exist external to, and independent of, us humans.
    Objects do not come to us with grid lines.
    Coordinatization is the mental act of imposing coordinates on objects to allow us to describe them, to analyze them, and to solve problems concerning them.
    Coordinatization is fundamentally human.
    We can and must <em>choose</em> coordinates.
    At first, this may seem like an extra burden, however it is in fact a powerful tool.
    When we confront a problem, we can and should reflect upon the situation and choose the coordinate system that enables us to most easily achieve our goals.
    This is what we do in calculus when we decide if we wish to approach the problem with Cartesian coordinates, polar coordinates, or something else altogether.
    This is the same thing we do in linear algebra.
  </p>
</insight>

<figure xml:id="fig-coordinatize-earth">
  <caption>The mental act of imposing coordinates on the Earth.</caption>
  <image source="images/5.4_coordinates_on_earth.png" width="100%">
    <description>Diagram showing the transition from an unlined globe to a coordinate system of longitude and latitude.</description>

  </image>
</figure>


<definition xml:id="definition-basis">
  <title>Basis</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>W\subset \vect{R}^m</m> is a subspace and <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots \vect{v}_n\}\subset W</m> is a collection of linearly independent vectors that spans <m>W</m>, then <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a <term>basis</term> for <m>W</m>.
      An <term>ordered basis</term> is a basis where we fix once and for all the order in which we list them.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p>
  The plural of basis is <alert>bases</alert>.
</p>

<!--
<sidebyside widths="50% 50%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
  <image source="images/5.4_coords1.png" width="100%"/>
  <image source="images/5.4_coords2.png" width="100%"/>
</sidebyside>

<sidebyside widths="50% 50%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
  <image source="images/5.4_coords3.png" width="100%"/>
  <image source="images/5.4_coords4.png" width="100%"/>
</sidebyside>
-->

<example xml:id="example-standard_basis">
  <title>Standard Basis of <m>\vect{R}^n</m></title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The columns of the <m>n\times n</m> identity matrix, <m>\mathcal{E}=\{\vect{e}_1,\ldots, \vect{e}_n\}</m>, is a basis for <m>\vect{R}^n</m>, which we call the <term>standard basis</term>. To see this, observe that if <m>\vect{v}\in \vect{R}^n</m>, then 
      <me>
        \vect{v} = \begin{bmatrix} v_1\\ v_2 \\ \vdots \\ v_n \end{bmatrix} = v_1\begin{bmatrix} 1\\ 0 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + v_2 \begin{bmatrix} 0\\ 1 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + \cdots + v_n \begin{bmatrix} 0\\ 0\\ \vdots \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = v_1\vect{e}_1+v_2\vect{e}_2+\cdots + v_n\vect{e}_n
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
  We now come to the Fundamental Theorem of Bases, which guarantees that, if we have a basis, we can make every vector in <m>W</m> with no redundancy.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="theorem-ftb">
  <title>Fundamental Theorem of Bases</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>W\subset \vect{R}^m</m> is a subspace, <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset W</m> is an ordered basis for <m>W</m>, and <m>\vect{w}\in W</m> is a vector, then there exists a unique collection of coefficients <m>x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n\in \vect{R}</m> such that
      <me>
        \vect{w}=x_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +x_n\vect{v}_n.
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      Since <m>\mathcal{B}</m> spans <m>W</m>, <m>\vect{w}\in \spn(\mathcal{B})</m>. Suppose now
      <me>
        \vect{w}=x_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +x_n\vect{v}_n,
      </me>
      and
      <me>
        \vect{w}=y_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +y_n\vect{v}_n,
      </me>
      for some <m>x_1,\ldots,x_n,y_1,\ldots,y_n\in\vect{R}</m>. Subtracting one from the other gives:
      <me>
        \vect{0}=(x_1-y_1)\vect{v}_1+\cdots +(x_n-y_n)\vect{v}_n.
      </me>
      The independence of <m>\mathcal{B}</m> (<xref ref="prop-dependence" />) implies each coefficient <m>x_i-y_i=0</m>, or in other words, <m>x_i=y_i</m>. We deduce that the coefficients are unique.
    </p>
  </proof>
</theorem>


<p>
  In linear algebra, it is bases that give us coordinates on subspaces.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-coordinate_vector">
  <title>Coordinate Vector</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>W\subset \mathbb{R}^m</m> is a subspace, <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset W</m> is a basis for <m>W</m>, <m>\vect{w}\in W</m>, and the collection of scalars <m>x_1,\ldots x_n\in \mathbb{R}</m> are the unique solution scalars for making <m>\vect{w}</m> with <m>\mathcal{B}</m>, then the <m>x_i</m> are the <term>coordinates of <m>\vect{w}</m> in the <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate system</term>, and
      <me>
        [\vect{w}]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix}x_1\\ \vdots\\ \ x_n\end{bmatrix}
      </me>
      is the <term><m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate vector</term> for <m>\vect{w}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p>
  The Fundamental Theorem of Bases (<xref ref="theorem-ftb" />) implies that any vector <m>\vect{w}\in W</m> has a <em>unique</em> <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-label.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-standard_coordinates">
  <title>Standard Coordinates on <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m></title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The standard basis <m>\mathcal{E}=\{\vect{e}_1,\ldots, \vect{e}_n\}</m> gives the <term>standard coordinates</term> on <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>. If <m>\vect{v}\in \mathbb{R}^n</m>, then
      <me>
        \vect{v} = \begin{bmatrix} v_1\\ v_2 \\ \vdots \\ v_n \end{bmatrix} = v_1\begin{bmatrix} 1\\ 0 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + v_2 \begin{bmatrix} 0\\ 1 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + \cdots + v_n \begin{bmatrix} 0\\ 0\\ \vdots \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = v_1\vect{e}_1+v_2\vect{e}_2+\cdots + v_n\vect{e}_n
      </me>
      so <m>[\vect{v}]_{\mathcal{E}} = \vect{v}</m>.
      The columns that we have been writing have been in standard coordinates this whole time!
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
  When conceptualizing column vectors as coordinates, or <q>labels,</q> the column <m>\vect{w}</m> and <m>[\vect{w}]_{\mathcal{B}}</m> are different labels we assign to a fixed vector. Moving between these labels is what I will call <term>change of coordinates</term> or <term>change of labels</term>.
</p>



<p>
  Suppose <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2\}\subset \mathbb{R}^2</m> is a basis.
  We will draw lines that help us visually measure vectors in units of <m>\vect{v}_1</m> and <m>\vect{v}_2</m>.
  For any integer <m>n</m>, the <m>n^{\text{th}}</m> <alert>constant-<m>\vect{v}_1</m> line</alert> is the line with parametric vector form <m>\vect{r}_n(t)= n\vect{v}_1+t\vect{v}_2</m>.
  Similarly, we have <alert>constant-<m>\vect{v}_2</m> lines</alert>, reversing the role of <m>n</m> and <m>t</m>.
  The constant-<m>\vect{v}_1</m> lines and constant-<m>\vect{v}_2</m> lines fill up the plane and form the <alert><m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate grid</alert>.
  The <alert><m>\mathcal{B}</m>-unit square</alert> is then the parallelogram determined by <m>\vect{v}_1</m> and <m>\vect{v}_2</m>.
</p>

<question xml:id="activity-bcoord-grid-reading">
    <p>
      Using the image below, with basis <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_2\}</m> as indicated in the image:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Identify and label the constant 0th, 1st, and 2nd constant-<m>\vect{v}_1</m> lines.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Identify and label the constant 0th, 1st, and 2nd constant-<m>\vect{v}_2</m> lines.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>What are the standard and <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates of <m>\vect{w}</m>?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Using what you know about the <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-grid lines, approximate the <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates of the vectors with standard coordinates <m>\begin{bmatrix}4\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{bmatrix}-3\\0\end{bmatrix}</m>, and <m>\begin{bmatrix}1\\3\end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <figure xml:id="fig-activity-grid">
      <image source="images/5.4_coordinates1.png" width="100%"/>
    </figure>
</question>

<example xml:id="ex-coordinates-visualization">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let us return to the example of <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_2\}=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix},\begin{bmatrix}-1\\1\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m> .
      <!--Let <m>P_{\mathcal{B}}=[\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_2]</m>.
      Since <m>\operatorname{rref}(P_{\mathcal{B}})=I</m>, Computational Quick Check <xref ref="cqc-basis" /> implies <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a basis.-->
    </p>
    <p>
      To draw the constant-<m>\vect{v}_1</m> lines, I draw the sequence of lines:
      <me>
        \vect{r}_0(t) = t\vect{v}_2, \quad \vect{r}_1(t) = \vect{v}_1+t\vect{v}_2, \quad \vect{r}_2(t) = 2\vect{v}_1+t\vect{v}_2, \quad \ldots, \quad \vect{r}_n(t) = n\vect{v}_1+t\vect{v}_2,
      </me>
      etc. for each integer <m>n</m>. In a similar way, I draw the sequence of constant-<m>\vect{v}_2</m> lines.
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="50% 50%" valign="middle">
      <p>
        The <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate grid and <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-unit square are plotted to the right.
        I have also plotted the vector <m>\vect{w}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\5\end{bmatrix}</m>. Observe, it lies on both the standard and <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate grids, and we can read off its coordinates. Make sure you can see that in <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates, <m>[\vect{w}]_{\mathcal{B}}=\begin{bmatrix}3\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>.
        <!--Our previous computations have shown,
        <me>
          \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1\amp-1\amp1\\1\amp1\amp5\end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1\amp0\amp3\\0\amp1\amp2\end{array}\right],
        </me>
        which symbolically corresponds to our visual observation above that <m>[\vect{w}]_{\mathcal{B}}=\begin{bmatrix}3\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>.-->
      </p>
      <image source="images/5.4_coordinates2.png" width="100%"/>
    </sidebyside>
  </statement>
</example>

<question xml:id="activity-coordinates-computation">
    <p>
      Consider the collection <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_2\}=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}3\\1\end{bmatrix},\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Verify <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a basis.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Sketch by hand the <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate grid and the <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-unit square.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Consider the vectors <m>\vect{u}</m> and <m>\vect{w}</m> such that <m>[\vect{u}]_{\mathcal{B}}=\begin{bmatrix}3\\-2\end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\vect{w}=\begin{bmatrix}4\\5\end{bmatrix}</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Plot them on your grid, and approximate <m>\vect{u}</m> and <m>[\vect{w}]_{\mathcal{B}}</m>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
</question>

<example xml:id="ex-coordinates-skew-grid">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Next, consider the example of <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_2\}=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}2\\1\end{bmatrix},\begin{bmatrix}-1\\1\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>.
      <!--Let <m>P_{\mathcal{B}}=[\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_2]</m>. Again, since <m>\operatorname{rref}(P_{\mathcal{B}})=I</m>, <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a basis.-->
      Its associated <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate grid and <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-unit square are plotted below.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the grid below, I have also plotted the vector <m>\vect{w}=\begin{bmatrix}3\\3\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Observe, it lies on both the standard and <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate grids, and we can read off its coordinates.
      Make sure you can see that in <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates, <m>[\vect{w}]_{\mathcal{B}}=\begin{bmatrix}2\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>. 
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-skew-grid">
      <image source="images/5.4_coordinates3.png" width="100%"/>
    </figure>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
    More generally, if <m>S\subset \mathbb{R}^m</m> is a collection of <m>n</m>-many nonzero, linearly independent vectors, then <m>S</m> is a basis for its span.  
    This gives a coordinate system on this span.
</p>

<example>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Consider the collection <m>S = \{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2\}\subset \mathbb{R}^3</m> where
        </p>
        <p>
            <m>\mathbf{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
            <m>\mathbf{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>.
            Let <m>W= \mathrm{span}(S)</m> be the plane spanned by <m>S</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.  Every point on this plane can be uniquely described by the amount of <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and the amount of <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>.  This gives a coordinate grid on <m>W</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            For example, consider the vector 
            <m>\mathbf{w} = \begin{bmatrix}3\\0\\5 \end{bmatrix}</m> lying in the plane <m>W</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            We can see we have <m>[\mathbf{w}]_S=\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>.  
        </p>
        <figure>
            <image source="images/5.4_coordinates4.png" width="100%"/>
        </figure>
        <p>
            Conversely, given <m>[\mathbf{u}]_S=\begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m>, this <em>means</em> <m>\mathbf{u} = 2\mathbf{v}_1-\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix}1\\5\\0\end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</example>


</section>